@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "AFTER UPDATE Trigger";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            AFTER UPDATE Trigger
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how AFTER UPDATE Triggers work in SQL Server,
            how to create them and how to use the
            <strong>inserted</strong> and <strong>deleted</strong>
            logical tables.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is an AFTER UPDATE Trigger?
        </h2>

        <p>
            An <strong>AFTER UPDATE Trigger</strong> is a special
            SQL Server database object that automatically executes
            after a successful <strong>UPDATE</strong> operation
            on a table.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is useful when we want to perform an automatic action
            whenever existing records are modified.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>It executes after an UPDATE operation.</li>
            <li>It can be used for auditing changes.</li>
            <li>It can store old and new values.</li>
            <li>It can maintain history or log tables.</li>
            <li>It can perform automatic database operations.</li>
        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic Syntax
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER TriggerName
ON TableName
AFTER UPDATE
AS
BEGIN

    -- Trigger logic

END;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Let's assume we have a <strong>Students</strong> table.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         INSERT SAMPLE DATA
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Sample Data
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    101,
    'Rahul',
    'Pune',
    21
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE HISTORY TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Create an Audit Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can create a separate table to store information
            whenever a student record is updated.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE StudentAudit
(
    AuditId INT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentId INT,
    OldCity VARCHAR(50),
    NewCity VARCHAR(50),
    OldAge INT,
    NewAge INT,
    ChangedDate DATETIME
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE AFTER UPDATE TRIGGER
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Creating an AFTER UPDATE Trigger
        </h2>

        <p>
            Now we create a trigger that automatically records
            the old and new values whenever a student is updated.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_StudentAfterUpdate
ON Students
AFTER UPDATE
AS
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO StudentAudit
    (
        StudentId,
        OldCity,
        NewCity,
        OldAge,
        NewAge,
        ChangedDate
    )
    SELECT
        d.StudentId,
        d.City,
        i.City,
        d.Age,
        i.Age,
        GETDATE()
    FROM deleted d
    INNER JOIN inserted i
        ON d.StudentId = i.StudentId;

END;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT LOGICAL TABLES
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            inserted and deleted Logical Tables
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides two special logical tables inside
            an UPDATE trigger:
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-light">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Logical Table</th>
                        <th>Contains</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>deleted</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Old values before the UPDATE
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>inserted</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            New values after the UPDATE
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         UPDATE OPERATION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Performing an UPDATE
        </h2>

        <p>
            Now update the student's city and age.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>UPDATE Students
SET
    City = 'Mumbai',
    Age = 22
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT HAPPENS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What Happens During UPDATE?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose the old record was:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>StudentId = 101
City = Pune
Age = 21</code></pre>

        <p>
            After the UPDATE:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>StudentId = 101
City = Mumbai
Age = 22</code></pre>

        <p>
            Inside the trigger:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>deleted</strong> contains Pune and 21.</li>
            <li><strong>inserted</strong> contains Mumbai and 22.</li>
        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CHECK AUDIT TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Check the Audit Record
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM StudentAudit;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The audit table will contain the old and new values
            generated automatically by the trigger.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE ROW UPDATE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            AFTER UPDATE Trigger and Multiple Rows
        </h2>

        <p>
            A trigger can execute for an UPDATE statement that
            modifies multiple rows.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Pune'
WHERE Age &gt; 20;</code></pre>

        <p>
            Therefore, trigger logic should be written using
            <strong>set-based operations</strong> rather than
            assuming that only one row is updated.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT NOTE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li>
                    AFTER UPDATE triggers execute after the UPDATE
                    operation.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>deleted</strong> contains the old values.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>inserted</strong> contains the new values.
                </li>

                <li>
                    A single UPDATE statement can affect multiple rows.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Always design trigger logic to handle multiple rows.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                An <strong>AFTER UPDATE Trigger</strong> automatically
                executes after a successful UPDATE operation.
                It is commonly used for auditing, maintaining history,
                tracking changes and performing automatic database
                operations. The <strong>deleted</strong> logical table
                contains the old values, while the
                <strong>inserted</strong> logical table contains the
                new values.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


